  {
    "id": "u00-l4",
    "title": "Spelling rules and where the tone mark goes",
    "summary": "A few rules explain the pinyin spellings that look odd at first.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "teach": {
          "title": "y and w",
          "body": [
            "When i, u or ü starts a syllable with no consonant before it, pinyin writes y or w: i → yi, u → wu, ü → yu.",
            "Longer ones follow suit: ie → ye, uo → wo, üe → yue."
          ],
          "contours": false,
          "items": [
            {
              "syllable": "yi1"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "wu1"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "yu2"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "yue4"
            }
          ]
        },
        "drills": [
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "How is the vowel i written on its own?",
            "options": [
              "i",
              "yi",
              "y"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "On its own, i is written yi (as in yī, one)."
          },
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "How is ü written on its own?",
            "options": [
              "ü",
              "yu",
              "wu"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "On its own, ü is written yu (as in yú, fish). wu is the u sound."
          },
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "How is üe written with nothing in front (as in “moon”)?",
            "options": [
              "üe",
              "ye",
              "yue"
            ],
            "answer": 2,
            "explain": "yuè (moon). ye is a different sound, ie."
          },
          {
            "type": "type",
            "syllable": "yi4"
          },
          {
            "type": "type",
            "syllable": "wu3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "teach": {
          "title": "ü after j, q, x",
          "body": [
            "After j, q and x, ü loses its dots: ju, qu, xu. It's still the ü sound, because j, q and x never go with a plain u.",
            "After n and l the dots stay, because nu/nü and lu/lü are different syllables."
          ],
          "contours": false,
          "items": [
            {
              "syllable": "ju1"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "qu1"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "xu1"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "nv3"
            },
            {
              "syllable": "lv3"
            }
          ]
        },
        "drills": [
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "How is j + ü written?",
            "options": [
              "jü",
              "ju",
              "jiu"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "ju: after j the dots are left off, but it's still ü."
          },
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "Which did you hear?",
            "options": [
              "lǔ",
              "lǚ"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "lǚ, with ü. After l the dots stay, because lǔ is a different syllable.",
            "audio": {
              "syllable": "lv3"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "type",
            "syllable": "ju1"
          },
          {
            "type": "type",
            "syllable": "xu1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "teach": {
          "title": "iu, ui, un",
          "body": [
            "Three spellings are shortened when a consonant comes first: iou → iu, uei → ui, uen → un.",
            "The full sound is still there: liù (six) has a little o in it, duì (correct) a little e."
          ],
          "contours": false,
          "items": [
            {
              "word": "w113"
            },
            {
              "word": "w044"
            },
            {
              "word": "w095"
            }
          ]
        },
        "drills": [
          {
            "type": "choose",
            "prompt": "l + iou is written…",
            "options": [
              "liou",
              "liu",
              "lio"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "liu, as in liù (six)."
          },
